WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.151 --> 00:00:04.535
<v Maya>Imagine stepping out of your office to the sound of a blaring fire alarm.</v>

2
00:00:04.856 --> 00:00:05.096
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

3
00:00:05.416 --> 00:00:11.322
<v Maya>You smell this acrid smoke in the hallway. You see the orange glow of flames licking the ceiling.</v>

4
00:00:11.422 --> 00:00:12.743
<v Daniel>Yeah, full panic mode.</v>

5
00:00:12.903 --> 00:00:20.571
<v Maya>Exactly. And then someone taps you on the shoulder and says, hey, relax, none of this is real. It is just an AI hallucination.</v>

6
00:00:20.751 --> 00:00:22.573
<v Daniel>Which is a wild thing to even think about.</v>

7
00:00:22.793 --> 00:00:37.729
<v Maya>It really is. But in today's digital landscape, that is exactly what is happening to our news feeds. We are basically standing in a hallway where our own senses and the safety sensors we built to protect us, well, they are fundamentally compromised.</v>

8
00:00:37.749 --> 00:00:38.850
<v Daniel>Completely compromised, yeah.</v>

9
00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:45.113
<v Maya>So welcome to this deep dive into the source material. Today, you and I are on a very clear, very urgent mission.</v>

10
00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:46.053
<v Daniel>Yeah, it is a big one.</v>

11
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:54.617
<v Maya>It really is. We're trying to figure out if anything or anyone can reliably catch an AI generated video of a real world crisis event.</v>

12
00:00:54.717 --> 00:01:00.420
<v Daniel>Right. And to do that, we're looking at a really fascinating, honestly, kind of terrifying stack of new research.</v>

13
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.261
<v Maya>Yeah. Terrifying is definitely the word.</v>

14
00:01:02.596 --> 00:01:10.660
<v Daniel>The absolute centerpiece for us today is a massive new paper. It literally just dropped three days ago on August 14th, 2026.</v>

15
00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:11.681
<v Maya>Hot off the presses.</v>

16
00:01:11.921 --> 00:01:22.146
<v Daniel>Exactly. It is this huge collaborative effort led by Xu Liang, Yixing Ma, and Pengfei Zhu. And it involves a massive 36-author team.</v>

17
00:01:22.246 --> 00:01:25.208
<v Maya>Wow, 36 authors. That is not your average paper.</v>

18
00:01:25.508 --> 00:01:36.755
<v Daniel>No, not at all. We were talking researchers spanning the National University of Singapore, UC Berkeley, ETH Zurich, Stanford, CMU, Zhejiang, Rendon, Shanghai Jiao Tong, and more.</v>

19
00:01:36.855 --> 00:01:38.496
<v Maya>They really brought out the heavy hitters for this.</v>

20
00:01:38.656 --> 00:01:42.038
<v Daniel>They had to. And they introduced this new benchmark called RA Bench.</v>

21
00:01:42.318 --> 00:01:47.161
<v Maya>Okay, our A-bench. And we also have five background papers we're bringing into the mix today, right?</v>

22
00:01:47.181 --> 00:01:55.826
<v Daniel>Yeah, those trace the recent history of AI video generation and detection. We're going to use them to understand the actual theories that our A-bench is putting to the ultimate test.</v>

23
00:01:55.986 --> 00:02:05.251
<v Maya>Perfect. So let us set the stakes for you, the listener, right now. Imagine a war zone or a devastating wildfire or a massive pandemic response.</v>

24
00:02:05.511 --> 00:02:06.592
<v Daniel>High stakes situations.</v>

25
00:02:07.003 --> 00:02:21.655
<v Maya>Exactly. A video of this hits your feed and you have to ask, is it real? Right. The terrifying answer from our A-bench is that, well, if it is a sophisticated fake, neither you nor the best AI detectors on the planet can reliably tell the difference anymore.</v>

26
00:02:22.128 --> 00:02:32.773
<v Daniel>It is a profound shift in what we consider truth. But, you know, to grasp why detection is failing so spectacularly today, we actually have to look backward first.</v>

27
00:02:32.873 --> 00:02:33.814
<v Maya>OK, let us rewind.</v>

28
00:02:34.094 --> 00:02:42.058
<v Daniel>We need to understand how video generators got this incredibly good and why early research gave the whole field this very false sense of security.</v>

29
00:02:42.238 --> 00:02:53.445
<v Maya>Right. Because teaching a computer to make a static image is one thing, but teaching it to generate video where lighting changes, objects move, physics has to actually make sense. That seems, I mean, infinitely harder.</v>

30
00:02:53.605 --> 00:03:01.130
<v Daniel>Oh, absolutely. And the turning point is mapped out in this crucial 2023 paper by Blattman and colleagues on stable video diffusion.</v>

31
00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:02.370
<v Maya>Stable video diffusion. OK.</v>

32
00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:08.714
<v Daniel>Yeah. They laid out this very specific three stage recipe that basically created the hyper realism we are seeing today.</v>

33
00:03:08.794 --> 00:03:09.955
<v Maya>And what are those three stages?</v>

34
00:03:10.275 --> 00:03:22.045
<v Daniel>First, they do text-to-image pre-training. That teaches the model what objects actually look like. Second is low-resolution video pre-training, where the model learns how things move over time.</v>

35
00:03:22.105 --> 00:03:23.526
<v Maya>Like basic physics and motion.</v>

36
00:03:23.806 --> 00:03:35.015
<v Daniel>Exactly. And then third is high-quality video fine-tuning. But the real secret sauce they argued for wasn't just the algorithm itself. It was massive data curation.</v>

37
00:03:35.253 --> 00:03:39.056
<v Maya>Right. So they didn't just scrape the entire Internet and dump everything into the machine.</v>

38
00:03:39.256 --> 00:03:44.581
<v Daniel>No, no. They filtered out static scenes, excessive text, really awkward camera cuts.</v>

39
00:03:44.861 --> 00:03:50.325
<v Maya>So they essentially fed the model a strict diet of pure, high quality cinematic motion.</v>

40
00:03:50.405 --> 00:03:59.993
<v Daniel>Yes. And that curated training is exactly why the realism being tested in an R.A. bench is so unbelievably potent. The generators aren't just guessing how a fire moves anymore.</v>

41
00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:01.813
<v Maya>They have internalized the physics of it.</v>

42
00:04:01.973 --> 00:04:04.554
<v Daniel>Exactly. They have seen millions of perfect examples.</v>

43
00:04:04.614 --> 00:04:12.056
<v Maya>Which naturally brings us to the detectors. Because, well, for a few years there, the academic community actually thought they had this fake video problem handled.</v>

44
00:04:12.296 --> 00:04:22.298
<v Daniel>They really did. Yeah. Back in 2020, Wang and colleagues published this paper, it was called CNN Spot, showing that AI-generated images were, quote, surprisingly easy to spot.</v>

45
00:04:22.578 --> 00:04:24.718
<v Maya>But there is a massive catch in that title, right?</v>

46
00:04:24.958 --> 00:04:28.619
<v Daniel>Yes. The full quote is surprisingly easy to spot. For now.</v>

47
00:04:29.318 --> 00:04:33.001
<v Maya>That, for now, Hedge, is basically the spine of this entire deep dive today.</v>

48
00:04:33.021 --> 00:04:43.129
<v Daniel>It really is. In 2020, they showed that one classifier, trained on just a single early generator program in this case, could successfully generalize across 11 other models.</v>

49
00:04:43.329 --> 00:04:46.011
<v Maya>Catching fakes from generators it had never even seen before.</v>

50
00:04:46.111 --> 00:04:55.759
<v Daniel>Right. And that founded this whole era of massive optimism in the field. Everyone just assumed that AI fakes would always leave behind some kind of universal, invisible fingerprint.</v>

51
00:04:55.879 --> 00:04:59.242
<v Maya>Like a digital smudge that the algorithms could always lock onto.</v>

52
00:04:59.302 --> 00:05:09.052
<v Daniel>Yeah, and that optimism just kept snowballing. By 2023, Oja and colleagues published a paper literally promising a, quote, universal detector.</v>

53
00:05:09.232 --> 00:05:09.973
<v Maya>UnivD, right.</v>

54
00:05:09.993 --> 00:05:17.560
<v Daniel>Exactly, UnivD. They used this complex method involving nearest neighbor and linear probing in a pre-trained CLIP feature space.</v>

55
00:05:17.918 --> 00:05:21.941
<v Maya>Okay, that sounds intensely technical. What does that actually mean for us?</v>

56
00:05:22.341 --> 00:05:31.987
<v Daniel>It basically means they argued they could detect unseen diffusion and autoregressive models just by analyzing how the data clustered together in this vast digital library.</v>

57
00:05:32.207 --> 00:05:32.788
<v Maya>And did it work?</v>

58
00:05:33.248 --> 00:05:42.174
<v Daniel>In those early lab tests, it worked beautifully. They beat prior state-of-the-art models by a huge margin like 15.07 MAP on unseen models.</v>

59
00:05:44.175 --> 00:05:59.161
<v Maya>Wow. But reading this with the benefit of hindsight, it is almost funny. It is like building a high tech burglar alarm in 2020 and confidently assuming it will catch all the thieves in 2026. Right. Totally missing the fact that the thieves</v>

60
00:05:59.201 --> 00:06:03.823
<v Maya>have figured out how to just walk straight through the walls. The thieves do not even use the doors anymore.</v>

61
00:06:04.023 --> 00:06:05.084
<v Daniel>Is the perfect analogy.</v>

62
00:06:05.284 --> 00:06:12.827
<v Maya>So I have to ask you directly, what did this promise of a universal detector actually turn out to mean when our bench finally put it to the test?</v>

63
00:06:13.217 --> 00:06:17.343
<v Daniel>It meant almost nothing. And this is exactly where our bench crashes the party.</v>

64
00:06:17.463 --> 00:06:19.787
<v Maya>The bill finally comes due on that for now hedge.</v>

65
00:06:20.183 --> 00:06:31.991
<v Daniel>Yes, because those older universal detectors were largely built and tested in academic vacuums. R.A. Bench built the ultimate stress test using the highest stakes content possible.</v>

66
00:06:32.211 --> 00:06:40.176
<v Maya>And I love the setup here. It is brilliantly devious. They didn't just grab random Internet clips of, you know, artificial landscapes or people dancing.</v>

67
00:06:40.236 --> 00:06:41.557
<v Daniel>No, they went straight for the throw. They</v>

68
00:06:41.597 --> 00:06:47.961
<v Maya>really did. They gathered 675 public source videos of real actual crises.</v>

69
00:06:48.141 --> 00:06:51.363
<v Daniel>Right. Wars, natural disasters, pandemic responses.</v>

70
00:06:51.543 --> 00:07:02.349
<v Maya>And then they scene split those into over 5,000 clips. And after two intense rounds of human review for seven reviewers, then four adjudicators, they whittled it down.</v>

71
00:07:02.609 --> 00:07:04.530
<v Daniel>Yeah, they were left with 1,830 verified real anchor clips.</v>

72
00:07:07.633 --> 00:07:14.078
<v Maya>Coming from 338 different sources across 10 social risk categories and 44 subcategories, it is exhaustive.</v>

73
00:07:14.238 --> 00:07:23.144
<v Daniel>And here is the design choice that makes R.A. Bench so lethal. They paired these real anchors with AI generations. But they didn't just type a random prompt into the machine.</v>

74
00:07:23.564 --> 00:07:28.348
<v Maya>No, they conditioned the image to video generation on the exact first frame of the real video.</v>

75
00:07:28.448 --> 00:07:35.353
<v Daniel>Exactly. They took the real first frame and added a highly detailed caption generated by Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview.</v>

76
00:07:35.676 --> 00:07:41.877
<v Maya>This is such a crucial detail because it perfectly mimics a real misinformation attack.</v>

77
00:07:42.177 --> 00:07:42.617
<v Daniel>How so?</v>

78
00:07:43.037 --> 00:07:51.719
<v Maya>Well, it is like a con artist showing you a perfectly valid ID to get inside your house. And only once they are inside do they start pulling off the heist.</v>

79
00:07:51.999 --> 00:07:52.499
<v Daniel>Ah, I see.</v>

80
00:07:52.779 --> 00:08:05.162
<v Maya>The AI uses that real first frame as the valid ID to anchor your trust. You see a real rocket on a launch pad or a real protest gathering. And then seamlessly, the AI hallucinates the disaster from there.</v>

81
00:08:05.442 --> 00:08:13.744
<v Daniel>That is exactly what happens. Yeah. So they ran this setup through four major open source generators, one 2.2 dynamic, one 2.2 lightning, LTX, and OmniWeaving. And</v>

82
00:08:13.784 --> 00:08:15.084
<v Maya>they didn't stop at open source.</v>

83
00:08:15.244 --> 00:08:22.166
<v Daniel>No. They also used five closed source heavyweights, Kling, Seedins 2.0, Runway, Hyluo, and Happy Horse.</v>

84
00:08:22.286 --> 00:08:25.267
<v Maya>So they are throwing the absolute best commercial models at this.</v>

85
00:08:25.667 --> 00:08:29.128
<v Daniel>Exactly. They generated 16,056 fake clips. That gave them a total of 17,886 videos to test.</v>

86
00:08:34.556 --> 00:08:45.427
<v Maya>So we have this massive gauntlet of hyper-realistic, anchored crisis fakes. How did those optimistic detectors, like CNN Spot and UniVFD, how did they hold up?</v>

87
00:08:45.567 --> 00:08:54.916
<v Daniel>It was a total collapse. They tested seven traditional detectors in total, CNN Spot, NPR, UniVFD, Forgelins, Decof, D3, and Restron.</v>

88
00:08:54.956 --> 00:08:56.658
<v Maya>And historically, what were their scores?</v>

89
00:08:57.038 --> 00:09:11.630
<v Daniel>On older data sets, their published reference AUCs, which is basically their ability to distinguish real from fake, were between 67.6 and 98.6%. So up in the high 90s for some. Right, but on our A bench. They fell to</v>

90
00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:15.091
<v Daniel>source-level means of 43.9% to 57.3%.</v>

91
00:09:15.151 --> 00:09:19.913
<v Maya>Wait, 43% to 57%? That is literally just random guessing. They dropped to coin flip territory.</v>

92
00:09:19.953 --> 00:09:29.256
<v Daniel>In some cases, they were statistically worse than a coin flip. And it wasn't just a uniform drop, either. The detector rankings completely reshuffled depending on which generator made the fake.</v>

93
00:09:29.536 --> 00:09:32.997
<v Maya>So a detector that caught Kling might totally fail on Seedence.</v>

94
00:09:33.138 --> 00:09:46.623
<v Daniel>Exactly. None of the three major detector families generalized consistently. The algorithms fundamentally failed. Because the new generators aren't leaving those old pixel smudges anymore. They are generating flawless physical motion.</v>

95
00:09:47.444 --> 00:09:54.296
<v Maya>Okay, so the algorithms completely fail. But, you know, you and I, humans, we have lived in the real world our whole lives.</v>

96
00:09:54.574 --> 00:09:56.156
<v Daniel>We have a lot of practice with reality.</v>

97
00:09:56.236 --> 00:10:03.846
<v Maya>Right. We understand gravity. We understand lighting, human anatomy. Surely we have a gut instinct for reality that a machine just does not have.</v>

98
00:10:03.986 --> 00:10:11.075
<v Daniel>You would really hope so. But R.A. Bench tested human perception, too, and the results are incredibly sobering.</v>

99
00:10:11.195 --> 00:10:12.077
<v Maya>Date me with the numbers.</v>

100
00:10:12.477 --> 00:10:18.786
<v Daniel>So reviewers were able to flag 68.6% of the fakes made by open source models.</v>

101
00:10:18.846 --> 00:10:22.070
<v Maya>Okay, almost 70%. Not great, but not terrible.</v>

102
00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:26.576
<v Daniel>But when it came to the closed source generators, human detection dropped to 52.9%. Oh,</v>

103
00:10:26.997 --> 00:10:28.359
<v Maya>wow. Back to a cornflip.</v>

104
00:10:28.539 --> 00:10:40.582
<v Daniel>Yeah. And for specific state-of-the-art models, it is even worse. For seedings 2.0, human detection fell to 40.7%. For cling, it was 45.1%.</v>

105
00:10:40.682 --> 00:10:42.462
<v Maya>We are failing the coin flip on those.</v>

106
00:10:42.743 --> 00:10:47.904
<v Daniel>We are. But honestly, the number worth dwelling on goes in the complete other direction.</v>

107
00:10:47.944 --> 00:10:48.324
<v Maya>What do you mean?</v>

108
00:10:48.524 --> 00:10:56.086
<v Daniel>The researchers found that 22.8% of genuine real crisis videos were judged by humans as being generated by AI.</v>

109
00:10:56.947 --> 00:11:08.980
<v Maya>Wait, let me wrap my head around that. 22.8%. Nearly a quarter of the time, we are looking at real footage of human suffering or actual real-world chaos, and our brains are rejecting it as a computer trick.</v>

110
00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.843
<v Daniel>Exactly. We are already disbelieving reality.</v>

111
00:11:11.863 --> 00:11:15.727
<v Maya>That is easily the most disturbing statistic in this entire stack of research.</v>

112
00:11:15.867 --> 00:11:24.311
<v Daniel>And it brings us to a massive collision with a widely accepted theory from a few years ago. Let's talk about the Grow 2022 paper published in PNAS.</v>

113
00:11:24.411 --> 00:11:27.392
<v Maya>Right. I remember this one. They had a huge sample size, didn't they?</v>

114
00:11:27.592 --> 00:11:35.016
<v Daniel>Massive. 15,016 participants. They tested ordinary people and the leading deep fake detection model at the time.</v>

115
00:11:35.176 --> 00:11:37.137
<v Maya>And what did they find back in 2022?</v>

116
00:11:37.397 --> 00:11:44.640
<v Daniel>They concluded that humans and machines are similarly accurate, but, and this is the key, they make entirely different mistakes.</v>

117
00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:49.823
<v Maya>Oh, I see where this is going. So the idea was a human machine team is the ultimate fix.</v>

118
00:11:50.103 --> 00:12:01.289
<v Daniel>Exactly. Machine-informed crowds beat either humans or machines alone. The machine catches the invisible pixel artifacts and the human catches the weird physics or illogical context.</v>

119
00:12:01.389 --> 00:12:02.529
<v Maya>A perfect symbiosis.</v>

120
00:12:02.689 --> 00:12:11.634
<v Daniel>That was the hope. But the new RA Bench paper completely dismantles this remedy. And they do it with a subset of their data called RA Bench Human Proof.</v>

121
00:12:11.838 --> 00:12:15.039
<v Maya>Human proof, that sounds ominous. What exactly is in that subset?</v>

122
00:12:15.059 --> 00:12:20.600
<v Daniel>They isolated 633 generated clips that successfully fooled all five human reviewers.</v>

123
00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:25.341
<v Maya>So every single person looked at these AI videos and confidently said, yes, this is real.</v>

124
00:12:25.621 --> 00:12:32.223
<v Daniel>Every single one. So the researchers took exactly these 633 human deceptive clips and fed them to the machines.</v>

125
00:12:32.612 --> 00:12:37.380
<v Maya>To see if the AI could catch what the humans missed, exactly like the Grow paper suggested.</v>

126
00:12:37.461 --> 00:12:42.029
<v Daniel>Exactly. And the results? The seven traditional detectors averaged a 47.5% AUC.</v>

127
00:12:44.444 --> 00:12:45.285
<v Maya>Worse than chance.</v>

128
00:12:45.365 --> 00:12:55.110
<v Daniel>Worse than random chance. Even the massive state-of-the-art models like Gemini binary and diagnostic only reached 54.7 and 54.5%.</v>

129
00:12:55.191 --> 00:12:57.972
<v Maya>Wow. So it completely breaks the growth theory.</v>

130
00:12:58.192 --> 00:13:02.715
<v Daniel>Completely. Human failure and machine failure perfectly overlap on these videos.</v>

131
00:13:02.895 --> 00:13:09.399
<v Maya>If the human deceptive videos are the exact same videos that deceive the detectors, it means our errors are correlated.</v>

132
00:13:09.499 --> 00:13:12.381
<v Daniel>Yes. That is the paper's absolute sharpest finding.</v>

133
00:13:12.625 --> 00:13:23.413
<v Maya>If our blind spot is exactly the same as the machine's blind spot, teaming up does not give us 20-20 vision. It just means we are both confidently walking off the same cliff together right when the stakes are highest.</v>

134
00:13:23.593 --> 00:13:35.842
<v Daniel>Exactly. The videos that fool people are the exact ones the machines miss. The generators have learned to mimic the very visual shortcuts that both our organic brains and the silicon brains rely on to determine reality.</v>

135
00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:41.432
<v Maya>And as if that wasn't bad enough, the study shows it actually gets much, much worse.</v>

136
00:13:41.592 --> 00:13:42.413
<v Daniel>Oh, it really does.</v>

137
00:13:42.553 --> 00:13:52.477
<v Maya>Because everything we just talked about, the terrible detector scores, the human failures, the correlated blind spots, all of that was achieved on pristine original video files.</v>

138
00:13:53.057 --> 00:13:55.478
<v Daniel>The raw MP4 files straight out of the generator.</v>

139
00:13:55.538 --> 00:14:00.180
<v Maya>But you and I both know that is not how you consume media on the Internet.</v>

140
00:14:00.365 --> 00:14:06.189
<v Daniel>Not at all. Videos online are compressed, re-shared, ripped, uploaded again. They are messy.</v>

141
00:14:06.329 --> 00:14:08.050
<v Maya>So how did the researchers test that?</v>

142
00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:18.056
<v Daniel>They built another subset called RA Bench Last Mile. They ran a social dissemination simulation over 150 anchors to mimic what happens when a video goes viral.</v>

143
00:14:18.216 --> 00:14:20.917
<v Maya>Like simulating a real-world sharing pipeline.</v>

144
00:14:21.098 --> 00:14:30.043
<v Daniel>Yes. They did a VP9 to H.264 transcode. They did a 0.5x downsampling. They dropped the frame rate to 8 frames per second.</v>

145
00:14:30.253 --> 00:14:36.575
<v Maya>So basically turning it into that slightly grainy, low res Internet sludge we are all so used to seeing on Twitter or Telegram.</v>

146
00:14:37.015 --> 00:14:44.858
<v Daniel>Exactly. And then for the final touch, they slapped a synthetic news badge like a little breaking news graphic right in the corner of the video.</v>

147
00:14:44.918 --> 00:14:50.760
<v Maya>Just a standard presentation cue. What did that whole last mile process do to the detectors?</v>

148
00:14:51.111 --> 00:15:03.958
<v Daniel>The drop was devastating. Combined, these simple edits dropped the mean fake recall across five fine-tuned detector configurations from 46% down to a microscopic 1.4%.</v>

149
00:15:04.638 --> 00:15:05.518
<v Maya>Wait,</v>

150
00:15:05.719 --> 00:15:06.839
<v Daniel>1.4%. 1.4%.</v>

151
00:15:07.159 --> 00:15:11.902
<v Maya>They went from feeling a coin flip to basically being completely blindfolded.</v>

152
00:15:12.042 --> 00:15:16.784
<v Daniel>It is total systemic failure. But the most fascinating part is actually just the news badge.</v>

153
00:15:17.024 --> 00:15:18.185
<v Maya>Right. Let's talk about that.</v>

154
00:15:18.445 --> 00:15:25.867
<v Daniel>The paper notes that just slapping that fake news logo on the video dropped the fake recall from 29.9% down to 14%. It cut</v>

155
00:15:26.068 --> 00:15:27.668
<v Maya>it in half, just a graphic.</v>

156
00:15:27.748 --> 00:15:36.091
<v Daniel>Yes. A presentation cue that makes zero change to the underlying pixels of the actual crisis event pushes the detectors toward calling a fake reel.</v>

157
00:15:36.271 --> 00:15:39.032
<v Maya>Why does that happen? It seems so foolish for an advanced AI.</v>

158
00:15:39.542 --> 00:15:54.351
<v Daniel>It reveals how inherently lazy machine learning models can be. They look for the strongest, easiest signal to reduce their error rate. Analyzing complex temporal consistency is hard, but recognizing a static high contrast</v>

159
00:15:54.371 --> 00:15:57.193
<v Daniel>news ticker, that is super easy.</v>

160
00:15:57.453 --> 00:16:03.816
<v Maya>So the detector sees the professional packaging and essentially says, ah, reputable news graphics. This must be real.</v>

161
00:16:04.156 --> 00:16:12.641
<v Daniel>Exactly. It judges the packaging, not the product inside. And sharing doesn't just weaken detection. It actually scrambles which detector looks best.</v>

162
00:16:12.921 --> 00:16:13.441
<v Maya>How so?</v>

163
00:16:13.561 --> 00:16:20.365
<v Daniel>The researchers checked the Spearman correlation between the detector rankings on the original pristine videos versus the fully disseminated videos.</v>

164
00:16:20.505 --> 00:16:21.505
